@meta
id: 11-15
title: Ampersand repeats the substitute
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: amp-repeat-sub, dot-repeat
par: 46
minutes: 6
vscode: replace-all, and then repair the ten comments it also changed
open: refund.test.ts

@brief
Ten assertions, each expecting `'GBP'`, each followed by a comment that
mentions GBP as a word. The ledger has moved to euros, so the ten
assertions expect `'EUR'` now. The ten comments are history and stay as
they are.

The pattern you need includes the quotes, which is not something the word
search you have been using can express. So this one is a substitute, and
the command is written out for you below — chapter 14 is where `:s` gets
explained properly.

What this lesson is about is the second line and the ninth. Run the
substitute once and then find out what `.` does with it.

@keys
| `:s/'GBP'/'EUR'/` `Enter` | on this line, replace the first `'GBP'` with `'EUR'` |
| `&` | run that same substitute again on the current line |
| `.` | does nothing useful here — `:s` is not a change `.` remembers |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
Run the command on the first assertion. Then move down one line and try
to repeat it — you will find the key you have been using all chapter is
not the one.
--
`.` repeats the last change to the text. `:s` is an ex command, and ex
commands are kept in a different place. The key for repeating the last
substitute on the current line is `&`.
--
`:s/'GBP'/'EUR'/` and Enter on the first assertion, then `j&` nine times.

@solution
    :s/'GBP'/'EUR'/<Enter>   then  j&  nine times

`&` is shorthand for "the last substitute, here". It reuses the pattern
and the replacement, but not the flags, so a `:s` you ran with `g` comes
back without it — one occurrence per line, which is exactly what this file
wants and is worth remembering as a trap in files where it is not.

The real content of this lesson is the division. Vim keeps two separate
memories of what you last did:

- `.` holds the last change made from normal mode. It knows nothing about
  the command line.
- `&` holds the last `:substitute`, whatever range or flags it ran with.

They do not overlap and neither one overwrites the other. You can make a
normal-mode edit, run a substitute, and `.` still holds your edit.

Which of them to reach for is mostly a question of what the pattern looks
like. When you can put the cursor on the thing, `*` and `cgn` and `.` will
be shorter and will show you every match on the way past. When the thing
is only describable as a pattern — with punctuation in it, or anchored, or
part of a word — a substitute states it once and `&` walks it down the
file. Chapter 14 makes the substitute do whole files at a stroke and you
will need `&` less, but it never stops being the cheapest way to say
"again, here".

@start refund.test.ts
describe('legacy refunds', () => {
  it('reports the ledger currency', () => {
    expect(refunds['ORD-1182'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP ledger, closes in Q3
    expect(refunds['ORD-1183'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP ledger, closes in Q3
    expect(refunds['ORD-1184'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // raised as GBP in 2023
    expect(refunds['ORD-1185'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // raised as GBP in 2023
    expect(refunds['ORD-1186'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP, card payment
    expect(refunds['ORD-1187'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP, card payment
    expect(refunds['ORD-1188'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP, bank transfer
    expect(refunds['ORD-1189'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP, bank transfer
    expect(refunds['ORD-1190'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP, manual adjustment
    expect(refunds['ORD-1191'].currency).toEqual('GBP'); // GBP, manual adjustment
  });
});

@expected refund.test.ts
describe('legacy refunds', () => {
  it('reports the ledger currency', () => {
    expect(refunds['ORD-1182'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP ledger, closes in Q3
    expect(refunds['ORD-1183'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP ledger, closes in Q3
    expect(refunds['ORD-1184'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // raised as GBP in 2023
    expect(refunds['ORD-1185'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // raised as GBP in 2023
    expect(refunds['ORD-1186'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP, card payment
    expect(refunds['ORD-1187'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP, card payment
    expect(refunds['ORD-1188'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP, bank transfer
    expect(refunds['ORD-1189'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP, bank transfer
    expect(refunds['ORD-1190'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP, manual adjustment
    expect(refunds['ORD-1191'].currency).toEqual('EUR'); // GBP, manual adjustment
  });
});
